Date: Wed, 12 Jan 94 21:01:09 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: the singularity; the "so-so" future (from mark Plus)

Mark Plus doubts whether there's much scope for more integration of the
global market place:

> (In case you doubt that really poor countries are already integrated
> into the world market, just check clothing labels in department
> stores.  Odds are you'll find garments made in Bangladesh, of all
> places.)  ....  So market expansion as a source of economic growth is
> reaching limits.

Goods are reaching us from all over the world.  But that doesn't mean
market globalisation is anywhere near complete.  Market growth is a two
way process.  We buy from them and they to buy from us.  Full
integration and the resulting economies of scale are reached when
foreign consumers have the same purchasing power as ours - ie when per
capita GDP is approx equal.  Consider:  As the world's largest economy
the US's is only 1/20 of the world's population.  50 years ago the US
was nearly 1/2 the world's GDP and, in modern trade terms, reasonably
isolated.  Now it is approx 1/5 of the world's GDP.  Still a long way
to go.  Decades, perhaps centuries, of market integration to go.

World GDP roughly tracks world trade.  During the inter-war recession,
for instance, trade and GDP collapsed together.  Since WW-II they have
grown together, falling back during recessions and spurting forward
during booms.  If this correlation remains then we can look forward to
long and continued market-lead growth.  All the signs are that the new
markets are opening up better than we had any right to hope for even
five years ago.  Unlike Mark, I see this not as the beginning of the end
but - you've guessed - the end of the beginning.

