Date: Wed, 12 Jan 94 21:31:35 GMT
From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: The singularity; the"so-so" future

Mark Plus:

>    I think a case can be made that we are witnessing the
> spontaneous limitation of economic growth in our (unextended)
> lifetimes, as evidenced in part by the plateauing of productivity
> and real income growth in the developed countries.

That's a pretty strong statement contradicted by the evidence,
especially the bit about real income growth.  One of the ways of
measuring average real income is to look at per capita GDP.  These
figures do not show any plateauing in the industrialised countries (even
less so in the lower-income countries, with the exception of Africa). 
Taking a decades broad view the post WW-II period has been one of
unprecedented growth, dwarfing all previous expansions*.

Moving to productivity levels let's suppose that growth is less dramatic
in the "West".  (I don't accept that it is - I just don't know.).  What
would such a slow down indicate?  That production is shifting to the
lower income countries, allowing their economies to grow more rapidly,
while the more industrialised countries concentrate on expensive value
added services.  This has happened in Japan and the first generation of
dragon economies, with production shifting successively offshore.  The
result is that their economies expand very rapidly and new markets are
created allowing increased globalisation and more growth, world-wide. 
So we win in the long run on this as well.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

     [* You may think that expansion was more rapid in, say, the
     opening up of the Wild West or industralisation of Victorian
     Britain - but it wasn't.  You must examine the per capita data
     to correct for immigration.  Last century per capita GDP (in
     industralised world) grwth was approx 1/2% pa.  Since WW-II 
     it's been in the 1-3% pa, with some less developed countries
     notching up much higher rates as they catch up.]


