I find much of your speculation probable or at least believable.
There are several issues which you might wish to explore or
emphasize further...

1) The possibility that uploading might not require the
destruction of the original biological brain.

2) The possibility that 'downloading' (into a [possibly cloned]
biological brain) might become feasible before, concurrently
with or after uploading.

3) The possibility that upload reproduction might offer
possibilities other than straight asexual copying:
'conventional' sexual reproduction, where the offspring
contains parts of 2 parent uploads, totalling the
equivalent of 1 in some size measure or trait count;
'extended' sexual reproduction, where the offspring
contains parts of 2 parent uploads, but totalling more
than the equivalent of 1 (like getting 1+ strand of DNA
from each parent, rather than exactly 1, per chromosome);
and 'orgiastic' sexual reproduction with N parents.

4) The possibility that biological brains might be
interfaced with and extended by computers to form
cyborgs which are neither true uploads nor true AIs.
I personally expect this first and find it more
attractive than pure uploading.  Although I agree
with you that a true human-level general purpose AI
is likely to follow development of the required
hardware by many years, I believe that better-than-
human specialist AIs are essentially here now.
I look forward to augmenting my brain with hardware
and software which gives me additional capabilities
like: mentally controlling huge cranes and micromachines
as if they were my bio-limbs; digitally signing,
encrypting and sending digital 'telepathic' messages;
and spawning and then re-integrating autonomous network
knowledge agents.

I personally plan to spawn huge numbers of copies of
myself as a virtual army, if uploading arrives in time
for me to use it.  I see lots of advantages (including
financial ones) in being the "first on my block" to
have oodles of copies of myself.
