Date: Thu, 13 Jan 1994 00:58:34 -0800 (PST)
From: more@chaph.usc.edu (Max More)
Subject: Terminology:  Trans- and Posthuman

One of the recent threads that caught my eye involved the definitions of
"transhuman" and "posthuman".  Romana helpfully quoted my article from EXTROPY
#8 "Human to Transhuman To Posthuman".  However, I've slightly modified
my view since then.  To quote from the conclusion to The Extropian 
Principles v.2.5:  

"These are principles not only of belief but of *action*.  We become transhuman
only when we have fully integrated these values into our lives, when we have 
consciously transformed ourselves ready for the future, rising above outmoded
human beliefs and behaviors.  When technology allows us to reconstitute
ourselves physically, genetically, and neurologically, we who have become
transhuman will be primed to transform ourselves into posthumans -- persons
of unprecedented physical, intellectual, and psychological capacity, 
self-programming, potentially immortal, unlimited individuals."

Here, unlike in the earlier article, I am not distinguishing transhuman from
posthuman simply in terms of the degree of technological modification to the 
body & brain.  A transhuman can still be entirely human in the genetic,
neurological, and physiological sense.  Transhuman is really a preparatory
phase (during which physical and neurological, etc., changes *can* take
place, but they are not crucial to becoming transhuman).  One becomes
transhuman by undergoing a Nietzschean process of "revaluation of all 
values"  - a process of questioning everything about yourself that comes
from the past, from the culture, and from unchosen biology.  This means 
becoming aware of who you are, and affirming or seeking to change each 
aspect of yourself.  (Of course some aspects you will not identify with
after conscious deliberation, but will be unable to change until 
the technology becomes available).

A posthuman is pretty much as I defined it in the article.  No one is yet 
posthuman.  Though you can be both human (biologically, etc) and transhuman
(psychologically and philosophically), you cannot be both human and posthuman.

One poster suggests that we should describe ourselves as "posthumanists"
rather than "transhumanists".  I disagree.  Transhumanists are those who
are committing themselves to growing beyond humanity, those who are in a 
transitional stage.  We cannot really become posthumanists until we can become
posthuman.  And, at that point, we will cease to talk of "posthumans" since 
we will have a more refined set of terms for distinguishing different types or
species of posthuman.

Boundlessly,

